          1018.0,
          788.0
        ]
      ],
      "provenance_score": 1.0,
      "content_sha256": "4da6f0905e93451704d65b1117c5991f9e4f047baa209d27159749b217f2641d"
    },
    {
      "type": "discussion",
      "content": "A simple way to test for nonlinearity, which avoids the pitfalls of regression, is to compare the change in mean phenotype (per increment in f) between two low levels of f and that between two high levels of f. Provided the two ranges of f are nonoverlapping, the two observed changes are statistically independent, even if the individuals at all four points in time are related. Letting the four observed mean phenotypes, in order of increasing f, be $ \\bar{z}_{1} $, $ \\bar{z}_{2} $, $ \\bar{z}_{3} $, and $ \\bar{z}_{4} $, a measure of nonlinearity is then given by",
      "source_page": 283,
      "printed_page": 263,
      "source_block_ids": [
        "Genetics_chapter10:p017:b5"
      ],
      "bbox": [
        50.0,
